***Doom 64*** is a 1997 [first-person shooter](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First-person_shooter "First-person shooter") game developed and published by [Midway Games](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midway_Games "Midway Games") for the [Nintendo 64](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nintendo_64 "Nintendo 64"). It is the second spin-off in [id Software](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Id_Software "Id Software")'s [*Doom* series](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doom_\(franchise\) "Doom (franchise)") after *[Final Doom](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Final_Doom "Final Doom")* (1996), and the fourth game in the series overall.

*Doom 64* was developed from 1994 by [Midway Studios San Diego](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midway_Studios_San_Diego "Midway Studios San Diego") under supervision of [id Software](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Id_Software "Id Software"), the main developer of the *Doom* franchise, and was tentatively titled *Doom: The Absolution*. It uses a customized version of the [*Doom* engine](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doom_engine "Doom engine"), enabling new kinds of level geometry, and dynamic colored lighting. The game features new [sprite](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sprite_\(computer_graphics\) "Sprite (computer graphics)") graphics for weapons and monsters.

*Doom 64* received positive reviews from critics, who praised its [graphics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Video_game_graphics "Video game graphics"), [level design](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Level_design "Level design"), soundtrack, and atmosphere, although some criticized the lack of new gameplay elements compared to the previous games of the series. It gained a [cult following](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cult_following "Cult following"), with several fan-made PC [source ports](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Source_port "Source port") and mods. A [remaster](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Video_game_remaster "Video game remaster") was developed by [Nightdive Studios](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nightdive_Studios "Nightdive Studios") and published by [Bethesda Softworks](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bethesda_Softworks "Bethesda Softworks") for [Nintendo Switch](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nintendo_Switch "Nintendo Switch"), [PlayStation 4](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PlayStation_4 "PlayStation 4"), [Windows](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Windows "Windows"), and [Xbox One](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xbox_One "Xbox One") in March 2020, and for [Stadia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Google_Stadia "Google Stadia") in May 2020.

## Gameplay

\[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Doom_64&action=edit&section=1 "Edit section: Gameplay")\]

[![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/d/d0/Doom_64_-_Gameplay.png/250px-Doom_64_-_Gameplay.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Doom_64_-_Gameplay.png)

A demon approaches the chainsaw-wielding player in Staging Area, the first level.

*Doom 64*'s gameplay is similar to that of earlier *[Doom](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doom_\(franchise\) "Doom (franchise)")* games. The player must advance through 28 story levels (and 4 secret levels) by battling demons, collecting weapons and keys, and activating switches to reach the level's exit while surviving deadly ambushes and traps. The *Doom* engine and gameplay elements were customized, and all visual assets such as weapon and monster graphics are unique to *Doom 64*.

### Weapons

\[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Doom_64&action=edit&section=2 "Edit section: Weapons")\]

All the weapons from *[Doom II](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doom_II "Doom II")* are present,[\[1\]](#cite_note-EGM92-3) but with new sprites and sound effects.[\[2\]](#cite_note-4) The [chainsaw](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chainsaw "Chainsaw") has two blades instead of one, the fists have bloodstained gloves instead of [brass knuckles](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brass_knuckles "Brass knuckles"), the [plasma gun](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plasma_weapon "Plasma weapon") has an electric core that emits a sparking sound, the [rocket launcher](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rocket_launcher "Rocket launcher") has a small kickback, the shotgun's priming handle is at the grip instead of under the barrel, and the double-barreled super shotgun reloads faster and causes kickback.

There is also a new weapon, unofficially called the Unmaker,\[*[citation needed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed "Wikipedia:Citation needed")*\] which uses [hitscan](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hitscan "Hitscan") but looks like a projectile weapon. It uses the same cell ammunition as the plasma gun and [BFG 9000](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BFG_9000 "BFG 9000"). It had originated in the *[Doom Bible](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doom_Bible "Doom Bible")* in 1992, and was planned for the original *Doom* in 1993.\[*[citation needed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed "Wikipedia:Citation needed")*\] Its appearance in *Doom 64* is its only official appearance prior to *[Doom Eternal](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doom_Eternal "Doom Eternal")*, in which it is spelled "Unmaykr". Three ancient artifacts hidden throughout the game's levels may be collected to upgrade the weapon. The first artifact increases the fire rate, the second artifact adds a second laser, and the third artifact allows the weapon to fire three simultaneous lasers which can automatically aim separately from one another, allowing the player to attack up to three different enemies at once with huge amounts of rapid damage.

### Enemies

\[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Doom_64&action=edit&section=3 "Edit section: Enemies")\]

Most of the enemies found in *[Doom](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doom_\(1993_video_game\) "Doom (1993 video game)")* and *[Doom II](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doom_II "Doom II")* are present in *Doom 64* with similar behavior and attributes. However, the revenant, arch-vile, spiderdemon, and "heavy weapon dude" enemies are not present.\[*[citation needed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed "Wikipedia:Citation needed")*\] Midway added one new monster to *Doom 64*, the nightmare imp, a variant of the imp from the original game. A unique [final boss](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boss_\(video_games\) "Boss (video games)"), the Mother Demon, was also created. Many of the existing enemies received substantial visual redesigns.

## Plot

\[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Doom_64&action=edit&section=4 "Edit section: Plot")\]

Following the [Doom Marine](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doom_Marine "Doom Marine")'s success at thwarting Hell in *[Doom](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doom_\(1993_video_game\) "Doom (1993 video game)")*, *[Doom II: Hell on Earth](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doom_II:_Hell_on_Earth "Doom II: Hell on Earth")*, and in the *[Final Doom](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Final_Doom "Final Doom")* storyline, a quarantine is established on the U.A.C. research installations with apocalyptic levels of radiation. For years, the installations stood abandoned, until a barely-functioning satellite monitoring one of them sends a message back to Earth. This message indicates that a "single [entity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Entity "Entity") with vast rejuvenation powers" was masked by the radiation and escaped detection. The entity was able to resurrect the demons by altering "decaying dead carnage back into corrupted living tissue". As the only experienced survivor of the *Doom* episodes, the Marine is sent in alone to exterminate them.[\[3\]](#cite_note-5)

However, the Marine realizes the demons have planned to lure him back into Hell. They are nonetheless unable to defeat him, and with the Unmaker, he eventually battles and kills the Mother Demon. The game ends with the Marine, no longer capable of having a normal life following his encounters with Hell's forces, deciding to remain in Hell forever to ensure no demon ever rises again.

In the *Lost Levels* from the 2020 remaster, the story continues as the Mother Demon was revealed to have a sister called the "Resurrector". It banishes the Marine back to a UAC base, forcing him to fight his way back to Hell to stop the Demons. It branches the storyline between the original games with the [2016 reboot of *Doom*](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doom_\(2016_video_game\) "Doom (2016 video game)") and *[Doom Eternal](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doom_Eternal "Doom Eternal")*.

## Development

\[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Doom_64&action=edit&section=5 "Edit section: Development")\]

*Doom 64* was developed by [Midway Games](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midway_Games "Midway Games") at its [San Diego](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Diego "San Diego") studio. [id Software](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Id_Software "Id Software"), the primary developer of the *Doom* franchise, supervised the project.[\[4\]](#cite_note-6) Development began in late 1994.[\[5\]](#cite_note-7) Its tentative title of *The Absolution* was changed to *Doom 64* for brand recognition, and reused as the name of the last level.\[*[citation needed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed "Wikipedia:Citation needed")*\] Midway wanted to include every demon from the original games into the final product, along with extra levels. However, deadlines and storage size constraints of the [Nintendo 64 Game Pak](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nintendo_64_Game_Pak "Nintendo 64 Game Pak") cartridges led them to them exclude the levels and a few demons.\[*[citation needed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed "Wikipedia:Citation needed")*\] Midway stated that a [multiplayer](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multiplayer "Multiplayer") mode was not included because [Nintendo](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nintendo "Nintendo") did not provide the necessary resources for multiplayer programming. Midway justified the decision based on alleged slowdown during split-screen multiplayer in other games on the console and the competitive nature of the mode. A Midway representative stated, "Everyone knows that the best part of playing multiplayer is not knowing where your opponent is and with a four-player split-screen, everyone can easily see where their opponents are."[\[6\]](#cite_note-8)

The environments were built from [3-dimensional polygon models](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polygonal_modeling "Polygonal modeling"), and the enemies were created by [pre-rendering](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pre-rendering "Pre-rendering") [sprites](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sprite_\(computer_graphics\) "Sprite (computer graphics)") with [SGI](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silicon_Graphics "Silicon Graphics") workstations.[\[7\]](#cite_note-NGen26-9) The Nightmare Imp was originally developed for the [PlayStation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PlayStation_\(console\) "PlayStation (console)") version of *Doom* and appeared in a near-complete beta of the game,[\[8\]](#cite_note-10) but was removed just prior to release for unknown reasons. It debuted in *Doom 64* instead.[\[1\]](#cite_note-EGM92-3)

*Doom 64* was slated to be a North American [launch game](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Launch_game "Launch game"), but near the deadline id Software expressed dissatisfaction with many of the level designs, so Midway postponed the game until April 1997 while redesigning the levels.[\[9\]](#cite_note-11) Nintendo's then-recent decision to remove the ability to run over animals from the Nintendo 64 version of *[Cruis'n USA](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cruis'n_USA "Cruis'n USA")* raised concerns about the possibility of censoring *Doom 64*, but Midway vice president of software Mike Abbot said Nintendo had not voiced any concerns about the game's violent content. He pointed out that *Cruis'n USA* was perceived by the public as a family game, while the *Doom* series was targeted towards mature gamers, making violent content less of a concern.[\[7\]](#cite_note-NGen26-9)

The music and sound effects were composed by [Aubrey Hodges](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aubrey_Hodges "Aubrey Hodges"), who had created the original sound effects and music for the PlayStation version two years earlier. The original *Doom 64* team was working on a potential sequel titled *Doom Absolution* designed only for two-player [deathmatches](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deathmatch_\(video_games\) "Deathmatch (video games)") soon after the first game was released, but canceled it.[\[10\]](#cite_note-12) Because id Software was impressed with the team's work on *Doom 64*, they were assigned to the Nintendo 64 version of *[Quake](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quake_\(video_game\) "Quake (video game)")* at this time.[\[11\]](#cite_note-13)

*Doom 64* was shipped to retailers by [Midway](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midway_Games "Midway Games") on March 27, 1997, and released on April 4.[\[12\]](#cite_note-14)[\[13\]](#cite_note-15)

