By the time *Doom 64* was released, the original *Doom* had been converted to nearly every platform capable of running it. Critics agreed that *Doom 64* was by far the best-looking *Doom* to date, exceeding even the PC version.[\[18\]](#cite_note-EGM94-20)[\[19\]](#cite_note-GSrev-21)[\[20\]](#cite_note-IGNrev-22)[\[24\]](#cite_note-NGen29-26)[\[27\]](#cite_note-GPro103-29) They were enthusiastic about the level designs, deeming them imaginative and much more challenging than those of the original *Doom*.[\[18\]](#cite_note-EGM94-20)[\[20\]](#cite_note-IGNrev-22)[\[24\]](#cite_note-NGen29-26)[\[27\]](#cite_note-GPro103-29) A *[Next Generation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Next_Generation_\(magazine\) "Next Generation (magazine)")* critic remarked that "even the most skillful *Doom* fans will have their hands full. And pushing door switches often causes whole rooms to rearrange and fold out into new shapes."[\[24\]](#cite_note-NGen29-26)

However, most reviewers said that the new graphics and levels were not enough to keep the game from feeling like yet another port of the original *Doom*.[\[19\]](#cite_note-GSrev-21)[\[20\]](#cite_note-IGNrev-22)[\[24\]](#cite_note-NGen29-26) [Peer Schneider](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peer_Schneider "Peer Schneider") of *[IGN](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IGN "IGN")* concluded, "Make no mistake about it, this is the best update to *Doom* so far—but if you've played the PC, PSX, SNES, Mac, Saturn, etc versions to death, you can do without this one."[\[20\]](#cite_note-IGNrev-22) *[GamePro](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GamePro "GamePro")* disagreed with the majority on this point, stating that "*Doom 64* pumps the tried-and-true corridor-shooter formula full of life, with another challenging, intense experience that showcases the system's capabilities." They rated it a perfect 5.0 out of 5 in all four categories: graphics, sound, control, and fun factor.[\[27\]](#cite_note-GPro103-29) Shawn Smith of *[Electronic Gaming Monthly](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electronic_Gaming_Monthly "Electronic Gaming Monthly")* instead regarded the lack of advancements in the basic *Doom* gameplay as a positive: "Some of you may want to see your space Marine jumping around or swimming underwater. Purists wouldn't want these features added because *Doom* wasn't about that stuff. I'll have to agree with the purists."[\[18\]](#cite_note-EGM94-20)

Most critics praised the game's musical score for its atmospheric effect.[\[18\]](#cite_note-EGM94-20)[\[19\]](#cite_note-GSrev-21)[\[20\]](#cite_note-IGNrev-22)[\[27\]](#cite_note-GPro103-29) Schneider and *GamePro* were both pleased with how well the analog control works,[\[20\]](#cite_note-IGNrev-22)[\[27\]](#cite_note-GPro103-29) but [Jeff Gerstmann](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jeff_Gerstmann "Jeff Gerstmann") of *[GameSpot](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GameSpot "GameSpot")* said it was off and said of the game overall, "On paper, *Doom 64* sounds better than the original could ever hope to be, but the end result feels more like a bastardization of the original."[\[19\]](#cite_note-GSrev-21) Comparing it to contemporary Nintendo 64 shooter *[Turok: Dinosaur Hunter](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turok:_Dinosaur_Hunter "Turok: Dinosaur Hunter")*, Schneider and *GamePro* both remarked that *Doom 64* has less freedom of exploration and depth of control, but is more intense and "anxiety-filled".[\[20\]](#cite_note-IGNrev-22)[\[27\]](#cite_note-GPro103-29)

*Doom 64* has garnered a [cult following](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cult_following "Cult following"), with multiple fan-made PC [source ports](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Source_port "Source port") compatible with PC [WAD files](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WAD_file "WAD file"). Most prominently, *Doom 64 TC* (2003) is a massive collaborative PC fan port led by Samuel Vilarreal to add numerous enemy variants and levels; and *Doom 64 EX* (2008) is a source port to re-create the original experience made by Vilarreal, who had worked on *Doom 64 TC* and a [Nintendo DS](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nintendo_DS "Nintendo DS") port of *Doom 64*. Patrick Klepek from *[Kotaku](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kotaku "Kotaku")* described it as the most underrated *Doom* game.[\[28\]](#cite_note-30) The game has also been praised for its unique level design that some consider the best in the series, and for its darker and more sinister tone, in contrast to the aggressive action of *Doom* and *Doom II*.[\[29\]](#cite_note-31)

## Remaster

\[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Doom_64&action=edit&section=7 "Edit section: Remaster")\]

A remaster of *Doom 64* for the [PC](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Personal_Computer "Personal Computer"), [Nintendo Switch](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nintendo_Switch "Nintendo Switch"), [PlayStation 4](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PlayStation_4 "PlayStation 4"), and [Xbox One](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xbox_One "Xbox One") by Nightdive Studios was released on March 20, 2020, and for [Stadia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Google_Stadia "Google Stadia") on May 12, 2020.[\[30\]](#cite_note-:0-32) It was included as a bonus for pre-orders of *[Doom Eternal](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doom_Eternal "Doom Eternal")*, which was released almost simultaneously.

The re-release includes *The Lost Levels* expansion, additional missions intended "to connect 'old' *Doom* to 'new' *Doom*".[\[31\]](#cite_note-33)[\[30\]](#cite_note-:0-32) The Doom Marine is forced out of Hell by the Resurrector, the Mother Demon's sister, and must fight his way back to Hell to slay the creature.[\[32\]](#cite_note-34)

